er of Miguel Moreno, from 1596 to 1655."}, {"sourceSlug": "miguel-moreno", "sourceName": "Miguel Moreno", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "francisco-de-rojas-zorilla", "targetName": "Francisco de Rojas Zorrilla", "context": "Miguel Moreno's works sometimes appeared in the same *Partes de Comedias* as those by Francisco de Rojas Zorrilla, suggesting shared theatrical circles."}], "places": [{"name": "Madrid, Spain", "role": "Primary Residence, Literary Hub"}, {"name": "Castile, Spain", "role": "Regional Context"}, {"name": "Seville, Spain", "role": "Publishing Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Miguel Moreno", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "miguel-moreno"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6844689", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Moreno"], "tags": ["Spain", "Political Leaders", "Europe", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Miguel Moreno contributed significantly to the vast dramatic and poetic output of the Spanish Golden Age, helping to define the stylistic and thematic contours of early 17th-century Spanish literature. His works, though less renowned than his peers, provide crucial insight into the period's literary breadth and public tastes.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:44:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40172087621", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Miguel Moreno (1596–1655) was a Golden Age Spanish poet and writer.", "newValue": "Miguel Moreno (1596–1655) emerged as a prolific and respected poet, playwright, and humanist during the zenith of the Spanish Golden Age, a period of unparalleled literary and artistic flourishing in Spain. Born in Madrid, Moreno's career unfolded amidst a vibrant intellectual landscape, deeply influenced by the theological and political currents of the Habsburg monarchy. His contributions to the *comedia nueva* and his lyrical poetry placed him firmly within the literary circles of early 17th-century Spain, engaging with the stylistic innovations and thematic explorations that defined the era.

Moreno's literary output included numerous *comedias*, *autos sacramentales*, and a significant body of lyrical verse, much of which was published in various *Partes de Comedias* compilations, alongside works by his more famous contemporaries. He was particularly known for his skillful handling of dramatic structure and his elegant poetic language, often exploring themes of honor, faith, and courtly love that resonated deeply with the Spanish public. His play, *La traición en la amistad*, is one notable example of his dramatic prowess, showcasing the intricate plots and moral dilemmas characteristic of the period's theatre.

